        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Behaviour · BEH·09</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Migrate?</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Residency, dispersal, and the difference between adults and juveniles</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> <strong>Most Common Ravens do not migrate</strong> — they are year-round residents. However, there are important exceptions and nuances: northern edge-of-range populations make seasonal movements; juvenile ravens are nomadic for 2–4 years before establishing territories; and even resident adults show local movements in response to food availability in winter.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Year-Round Residency: The Norm</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Across most of the Common Raven's Holarctic range, territorial adult pairs are year-round residents:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>They hold and defend territories in all seasons, not just during breeding</li>
      <li>Established pairs are present in the same area throughout the year</li>
      <li>This is the norm across the British Isles, Central Europe, North America (excluding far north), and most of the range</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Year-round residency makes ecological sense: maintaining a territory and knowing the local food sources through winter is more productive than the energy cost of migration.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Northern Populations: Partial Migration</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At the northern edge of the range, some populations show southward movements in winter:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>High Arctic Canada and northernmost Alaska: ravens move south as prey availability decreases in extreme winter</li>
      <li>Northern Fennoscandia: some movement southward from high-latitude populations</li>
      <li>These are partial migrations — not all individuals move; some remain year-round even at high latitude near human settlements (food subsidies from towns and military bases)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The trigger is food availability, not day length alone — ravens in areas with reliable human food sources (military bases, remote communities) may remain year-round at high latitude while birds in adjacent wild areas move south.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Juvenile Dispersal and Nomadism</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The most widespread form of raven "movement" is juvenile dispersal, not seasonal migration:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Young ravens leave their parents' territory at approximately 6 months of age</li>
      <li>They spend 2–4 years as nomadic non-breeders before establishing a territory</li>
      <li>During this period they may travel tens to hundreds of kilometres from their natal area</li>
      <li>They join communal roosts and move with other juveniles in search of food</li>
      <li>Marked birds from Maine studies have been recovered hundreds of miles from banding locations</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This juvenile nomadism is distinct from seasonal migration — it is developmental dispersal, and the birds eventually settle into permanent territories once they can establish one (by inheritance, displacement, or new territory formation).<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Winter Movements of Adults</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Even resident adult ravens may show local movements in response to food availability:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>When a large food source appears (a large carcass, an opened landfill, a salmon run), ravens from a wide area may converge and feed for days or weeks before returning to their home territories</li>
      <li>These movements are opportunistic and food-driven, not migratory in the strict sense</li>
      <li>At large carcasses in winter, ravens may travel 20–50 km from their territory for a single feeding bout</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This flexibility — the combination of year-round residency for adults and nomadism for juveniles — is part of what makes the raven such a successful wide-range generalist.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Madge, S. &amp; Burn, H. (1994). <em>Crows and Jays</em>. Christopher Helm. — movement patterns; northern populations.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Boarman, W. I. &amp; Heinrich, B. (1999). Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). <em>The Birds of North America</em>, No. 476. — juvenile dispersal; adult residency; banding recoveries.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> field-guide and species-account data</p>
  </section>

